Lights Out: presentation notes (That's the basis we used to show this in the video)
Summary :
When a storm hits the United States, some counties lose power for hours and others for weeks. We used 672 storms (2012 to 2026) to ask two questions: will a county have a major outage, and how long will recovery take?

Major outage: predictable, but only moderately. Random forest, AUC 0.71 on storms it never saw.
Recovery time: much harder. R² 0.05 when the storm hits, 0.21 once the size of the outage is known.
Satellite night light alone: not reliable. AUC 0.56, barely above a coin flip.
Method: split by whole storm, otherwise the scores look better than they are.
Notes by slide
1. Title
Predict storm outages and recovery, county by county
2. The problem
Damage is very uneven, and crews are sent before it is known
Question 1: more than 10% of customers out at the peak? (classification)
Question 2: hours from the peak until 90% are back? (regression)
One prediction = one county during one storm
3. The data
672 FEMA-declared storms, 51,493 storm × county rows
Two views: utility reports (EAGLE-I) and satellite night light (NASA Black Marble)
Usable rows: 9,989 for question 1, 7,297 for question 2
4. The targets

49.6% of rows are major outages: two balanced classes
Recovery: median 30 h, maximum 1,009 h, so we predict log(hours)
1,165 counties not restored within 45 days: true time unknown (censored)
5. The method

Only information known at prediction time
20% of storms kept for the final test, 5 grouped folds for the rest
Simple baselines first, then five models, best one tested once
Random split would give AUC 0.79 instead of 0.70, R² 0.25 instead of 0.19
6. Major outage: results

Night light 0.56, storm-type table 0.61, random forest 0.72 (cross-validation)
Final test on 93 new storms: 0.707
Every model except Naive Bayes beats the simple rules
7. How useful is it?

Threshold 0.5: finds 673 of 951 major outages (71%), 383 false alarms
Threshold 0.4: 133 more found, 239 more false alarms
Best use is a ranking: 72% of the 10% riskiest counties had a major outage, against 48% at random
8. Where it fails

Major outages found: severe storms 88%, tropical 65%, floods 24%, snow 7%
Errors come by whole storms: 8 storms at zero, 7 of them floods
Without the year: AUC 0.68. On storms from 2023: about 0.68. Retrain every season
9. Recovery time

Storm information only: R² 0.05
With the size of the outage at its peak: R² 0.21, median error 16 h (22 h for the average)
Missing: storm strength, network damage, crews, blocked roads
10. Hurricane Ida (2021)

193 counties, 78 major outages, never seen in training
Inside the wind radius: 33 of 33 found, 8 false alarms
Outside: 0 of 45 at threshold 0.5. At 0.4, 16 are found
Reason: no wind speed or rainfall data
11. The satellite

New Orleans and Houma almost dark five days after landfall
Baton Rouge looks normal, yet 69% of its customers were out at the peak
It only sees outages still going on, and clouds hide the nights between

12. Conclusion and next steps
Add storm strength (NOAA HURDAT2)
Survival analysis for the censored counties
Retrain every season, choose the threshold from the cost of a missed outage

